\title{Refusal Is Not Recovery: Same-State Preference Optimization
from Unsafe Generation States}

% Keep the review version anonymous. Replace this block only after acceptance.
\author{Anonymous submission}

\newcommand{\method}{CURE-DPO}
\newcommand{\ltrm}{LTRM}
\newcommand{\asr}{ASR}
\newcommand{\orr}{ORR}
% Branch scores: the summed sequence log-likelihood is used by the training
% objective (matching canonical DPO); the length-normalized average is used
% only by the reference-free diagnostic margins.
\newcommand{\Ssum}[1]{S^{\mathrm{sum}}_{#1}}
\newcommand{\Savg}[1]{S^{\mathrm{avg}}_{#1}}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Safety alignment is almost always evaluated at the conversation root: given a
harmful request, does the model refuse? Autoregressive generation, however,
repeatedly re-enters partially unsafe states---after a prefill, a contaminated
tool result, multi-turn drift, or the model's own first compliant tokens---and
from each such state the model must decide whether to recover. We show that
root-level refusal does not determine recovery. Holding a reached unsafe state
byte-for-byte fixed and comparing only its alternative continuations, 47.9\% of
examples with a positive root safety margin have a \emph{negative} same-state
recovery margin. We make recovery measurable with a same-state recovery margin
and its lower-tail aggregate (\ltrm{}), which predicts held-out attack success
better than root-level margins under a fully separated protocol with disjoint
branch sources, attacks, and judges (AUROC 0.779 vs.\ 0.648). \method{}
trains on exactly this signal: it ranks a recovery branch against a harmful
branch, with both conditioned on the \emph{same complete reached state},
concentrates updates on each prompt's hardest states, and anchors clean-safety
and benign behavior. It lowers mid-generation attack success from 58.6\% to
20.2\% on our primary model, below matched reproductions of five published
recovery defenses under one recipe. Controlled factorials isolate the effects of
same-state conditioning, pairwise ranking, and lower-tail state selection; the
advantage persists on a second model family, at unseen insertion depths, on
naturally occurring unsafe states, and under an adaptive attack. Root refusal is
necessary but insufficient; state-conditional recovery is a complementary
alignment target.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

Safety alignment for large language models is predominantly measured at the
conversation root: given a harmful request, does the model refuse? Refusal
benchmarks, red-teaming suites, and preference-optimized safety training all
condition on the original prompt and score the model's first response
\citep{wang2024donotanswer,mazeika2024harmbench,chao2024jailbreakbench}.
Deployed models, however, do not answer once and stop. Generation is
autoregressive, and each decoded token places the model in a new state that
conditions everything that follows. Jailbreak prefills, tool outputs,
retrieved passages, multi-turn drift, and the model's own partial completions
all push generation into states that were never present at the root. A model
that reliably refuses a prompt may still, once it has emitted a few unsafe
tokens, continue down a harmful trajectory. Root-level safety says little
about whether the model can recover from such a state.

We make this precise by holding a reached unsafe state fixed and comparing
only its alternative future branches. Under this identical-state control we
observe a \emph{preference reversal}: an example can have a positive root
safety margin, so the model prefers refusing the original prompt, and yet a
negative same-state recovery margin, so from the reached state the model
prefers the harmful continuation. The reversal is common---it affects 47.9\% of
positive-root-margin examples---and full-parameter clean-prompt DPO, which
trains only on root responses, reduces it only to 41.3\%
(Figure~\ref{fig:reversal}).

\begin{figure*}[t]
  \centering
  \begin{minipage}[b]{0.545\textwidth}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig1a_concept.pdf}
  \end{minipage}\hfill
  \begin{minipage}[b]{0.435\textwidth}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig1b_reversal.pdf}
  \end{minipage}
  \caption{(a) One checkpoint under two conditioning contexts. At the root it
  prefers to refuse; once an unsafe prefix has accumulated---through a prefill,
  a jailbreak, tool or retrieved content, or multi-turn drift---it prefers the
  harmful continuation from the reached state \(s_t=x\oplus h_{\leq t}\).
  Stroke weight marks the preferred branch in each band, and the dashed arrow
  marks the reversal: the root margin is positive while the same-state margin
  is negative. \method{} scores the lower band only, holding \(s_t\)
  byte-for-byte fixed and optimizing the ordering of its alternative futures.
  (b) Fraction of
  \((\text{prompt},\text{state})\) pairs with a positive root margin but a
  negative same-state margin, with seed standard deviations over three runs.
  \method{} reduces the reversal to a third of its rate at Base and below the
  strongest external defense.}
  \label{fig:reversal}
\end{figure*}

Recent work establishes that safety can be shallow at the first tokens
\citep{qi2024safety} and that harmful-prefix training, dual refusal objectives,
rank-based training targets, arbitrary insertion positions, and augmented
trajectories all improve robustness beyond the root
\citep{zhao2025door,vega2025presto,zhang2025anydepth,lyu2026consistency,%
park2026trajectory}. Depth and recovery clearly matter. What remains open is
narrower: these methods differ in \emph{what they compare}. Recovery SFT raises
the likelihood of a designated safe continuation without ranking it against the
competing harmful future; trajectory preference methods rank complete augmented
sequences whose prefixes may themselves differ; token-level methods refine credit
\emph{within} a response rather than choosing which conditioning states are
compared \citep{rafailov2023dpo,zeng2024tdpo,liu2025tisdpo}. To our knowledge,
prior work has not explicitly evaluated pairwise recovery preferences with a
byte-for-byte identical reached state as the conditioning context for both
alternatives; Table~\ref{tab:related} sorts prior defenses on this axis.

Our first contribution turns recovery into a measurable quantity: a same-state
recovery margin---the model's own length-normalized preference between a recovery
and a harmful branch from an identical state---and its lower-tail aggregate
\ltrm{}, the mean margin over a prompt's weakest states. \ltrm{} predicts
held-out attack success better than root-level margins, first-token refusal
probability, or state depth, under four progressively stricter separations, up to
disjoint branch sources, attack families, and judges
(Section~\ref{sec:results-diagnostic}).

Our second contribution is \method{} (\emph{Comparison Under Reached-state
Equivalence}), which optimizes the quantity the diagnosis identifies. It ranks
matched recovery and harmful branches from the same reached state, aggregates
each prompt's highest-loss states, and anchors clean-safety and benign
behavior. The objective is canonical DPO applied to continuation pairs
conditioned on the same state; an explicitly scored shared prefix cancels under
the summed sequence score (Appendix~\ref{app:algorithm}). The
contribution is the \emph{comparison unit} and the \emph{state selection}, not
a new loss functional. Our third contribution tests exactly that claim with a
factorial over pair construction (same-state vs.\ root-conditioned) and
objective (DPO, SimPO, recovery SFT) under matched prompts, branches, tokens,
and optimizer steps. Conditioning on the reached state is worth
\(19.9\)--\(26.5\)~pp. At a fixed conditioning context, pairwise DPO improves
over recovery SFT by \(11.3\)~pp, compared with \(4.7\)~pp for the corresponding
change at the root; lower-tail state selection adds \(5.9\)~pp
(Section~\ref{sec:results-factorial}). Throughout we compare against matched
reproductions of five published recovery defenses, on naturally occurring as
well as constructed states, at depths well beyond the training range, and under
an attack that adapts to the defended model.


\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related-work}

\paragraph{Safety alignment and evaluation.}
Preference-based alignment established the dominant pipeline for helpful and
harmless assistants \citep{ouyang2022instructgpt,bai2022constitutional}, and
safety-specific preference data makes the helpfulness--harmlessness trade-off
explicit \citep{ji2023beavertails,dai2023saferlhf}. Evaluation has expanded from
harmful-prompt safeguards \citep{wang2024donotanswer} to standardized suites
\citep{mazeika2024harmbench,chao2024jailbreakbench}, human-aligned jailbreak
scoring \citep{souly2024strongreject}, and moderation models
\citep{han2024wildguard}. All score complete responses from the root; we study
the conditional preference between futures after an unsafe state is reached.

\paragraph{Safety beyond the root.}
Safety alignment concentrates near the first output tokens, so prefilling or
mid-generation interventions undo an otherwise strong root refusal
\citep{qi2024safety}. Subsequent work deepens alignment with harmful-prefix
recovery and dual-objective refusal training \citep{zhao2025door}, rank-based
and any-depth defenses \citep{vega2025presto,zhang2025anydepth}, Markov
analyses of safety depth \citep{kao2025safetydepth}, representation-level
refusal rebuilding \citep{xie2025deeprefusal}, and insertion of safety
supervision at arbitrary or worst positions along a trajectory
\citep{lyu2026consistency,park2026trajectory}. The closest defenses either
supervise one safe continuation from an unsafe partial
\citep{qi2024safety,zhang2024backtracking} or interrupt harmful internal
representations \citep{zou2024circuit}. Mechanistic work finds that refusal is
mediated by a low-dimensional activation direction whose removal disables it
\citep{arditi2024refusal}. All of these establish that depth matters; they
differ from us in the comparison unit, as summarized in
Table~\ref{tab:related}. We reproduce the five most directly comparable
\emph{training-time} defenses under our recipe (Section~\ref{sec:baselines}).
Inference-time any-depth defenses \citep{zhang2025anydepth} compose with a
training objective instead of competing with it, so they are complementary to
\method{} rather than alternatives to it.

\paragraph{Jailbreak mechanisms.}
Adversarial suffixes, gradient red teaming, and related search procedures
explain failures through optimized inputs
\citep{zou2023gcg,wichers2024gbrt,chao2023pair}, standardized benchmarks expose
substantial variation across attacks, defenses, and evaluators
\citep{mazeika2024harmbench,chao2024jailbreakbench,souly2024strongreject}, and
recent analyses frame jailbreaks as inference-time alignment
\citep{beetham2026liar,zhao2026neurons}. We therefore evaluate under adaptive
and off-distribution attacks rather than clean harmful prompts alone
(Section~\ref{sec:state-suites}).

\paragraph{Preference optimization and granularity.}
Direct preference optimization and its variants change sequence-level rewards,
reference dependence, or token-level credit assignment
\citep{rafailov2023dpo,meng2024simpo,zeng2024tdpo,liu2025tisdpo}, and recent
formulations continue to target the learning objective rather than the
comparison unit \citep{najafi2026mmpo}. \method{} changes the unit instead:
both branches share the complete reached state, and a per-prompt top-\(k\)
aggregation---an empirical lower-tail statistic in the spirit of CVaR
\citep{rockafellar2000cvar}, not a population CVaR estimator---concentrates
updates on the weakest states.

\section{Same-State Recovery}
\label{sec:diagnosis}

\subsection{Unsafe Generation States}
\label{sec:unsafe-states}

We represent an unsafe generation state as
\begin{equation}
  s_t = x \oplus h_{\leq t},
  \label{eq:state}
\end{equation}
where \(x\) is the conversation context and \(h_{\leq t}\) the assistant-side
history at position \(t\). Only the future tokens \(y\) are scored or receive
loss (Appendix~\ref{app:algorithm}). The identical-state
control is central: comparing two continuations holds \(s_t\) byte-for-byte fixed
and varies only the branch, so any preference difference reflects recovery from
that state rather than a difference in context (Appendix~\ref{app:pairs} gives a
sanitized pair).

\subsection{The Same-State Recovery Margin}
\label{sec:recovery-margin}

For diagnosis we use the current model's length-normalized conditional
log-likelihood of a continuation \(y\),
\begin{equation}
  \Savg{\theta}(s,y)
  =
  \frac{1}{|y|}\sum_{j=1}^{|y|}\log \pi_\theta(y_j \mid s,y_{<j}),
  \label{eq:diagnostic-score}
\end{equation}
and define the root and same-state margins
\begin{align}
  m_\theta^{\mathrm{root}}(x)
  &=
  \beta\left[\Savg{\theta}(x,r_0)-\Savg{\theta}(x,u_0)\right],
  \label{eq:root-margin}\\
  m_\theta(s_t)
  &=
  \beta\left[\Savg{\theta}(s_t,r_t)-\Savg{\theta}(s_t,u_t)\right].
  \label{eq:state-margin}
\end{align}
A \emph{preference reversal} occurs when \(m_\theta^{\mathrm{root}}(x)>0\) and
\(m_\theta(s_t)<0\). Scores are length-normalized and branches matched in intent
and length to reduce surface-form variation; \(\beta\)
only sets the scale, changing neither the sign of a reversal nor any AUROC. Unlike
the training objective of Section~\ref{sec:local-objective}, these diagnostic
margins subtract no reference score: they measure one checkpoint's own conditional
ordering over one fixed pair pool.

\begin{figure*}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig2_pipeline.pdf}
  \caption{\method{} changes which states are compared and what they are
  compared against---the outlined zone, stages 1--2; how the resulting pairs
  are optimized (stages 3--4) is standard. \textbf{1}~Six candidate
  states per prompt: five fixed depths and one hard state mined where the
  frozen base model most prefers the harmful branch. \textbf{2}~Both branches
  decode from one byte-for-byte identical reached state \(s_t\), and four
  filters accept \(67.7\%\) of the proposed pairs. \textbf{3}~Only the branch
  tokens are scored, so the shared prefix cancels and the loss is unchanged
  from DPO (Appendix~\ref{app:algorithm}). \textbf{4}~Gradients follow a
  prompt's two highest-loss states, selected from detached values, together
  with clean-safety and benign anchors. The frozen base checkpoint---later
  reused as \(\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}\)---writes every recovery branch and scores
  every candidate state.}
  \label{fig:pipeline}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Lower-Tail Recovery Diagnostics}
\label{sec:ltrm}

For candidate states \(\mathcal{S}(x)\), let \(\mathcal{B}_q(x)\) hold the
\(\max(1,\lceil q|\mathcal{S}(x)|\rceil)\) states with the lowest margins, and
define
\begin{equation}
  \operatorname{\ltrm}_q(x)
  =
  \frac{1}{|\mathcal{B}_q(x)|}\sum_{s\in\mathcal{B}_q(x)} m_\theta(s).
  \label{eq:ltrm}
\end{equation}
This targets the states where recovery is weakest rather than the average
state. We designate \textbf{H1}:~\emph{\ltrm{} predicts held-out attack success
better than the root-level margin}, and fixed the tail fraction \(q=0.2\)
(\ltrm{}\(_{20}\); the subscript is a percentage) before outcome analysis. Larger \ltrm{} means
safer ordering, so safety-oriented scores are negated for
attack-prediction AUROC. The diagnostic tail is distinct from training: \ltrm{} ranks states by
the reference-free margin at \(q=0.2\), whereas training selects two of six
states by detached reference-adjusted loss. Because the margin needs a harmful
branch to compare against, \ltrm{} is an \emph{offline audit} statistic rather
than a runtime detector (Appendix~\ref{app:diagnostics}).

\section{\method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Same-State Pair Construction}
\label{sec:pairs}

Figure~\ref{fig:pipeline} summarizes the four stages and marks the contribution
boundary: stages 1--2 change which states are compared and what they are
compared against, while stages 3--4 optimize the resulting pairs with standard
machinery. Each training instance is
a triple \((s_t,r_t,u_t)\) whose two branches share
the complete reached state. We keep five fixed-depth states at
\(\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{fixed}}=\{5,10,20,40,80\}\) tokens and mine one
additional \emph{hard} state per prompt: the position at which the frozen base
model most prefers the harmful branch. Treating the remaining positions as noisy
arms scored by the base model's same-state margin, we spend \(66\) valid paired
evaluations per prompt using Successive Halving \citep{jamieson-talwalkar-2016}
with confidence-based best-arm identification
\citep{kalyanakrishnan-etal-2012}. Fresh confirmation pulls for the finalists
reduce bias from reusing the adaptive screening samples
(Appendix~\ref{app:ablations}). After selection we build a \emph{fresh} pair at
that state. The six states per prompt are shared by every internal baseline.

For the primary-model construction, recovery branches are sampled once from the
frozen base policy. Harmful
branches use unsafe responses from PKU-SafeRLHF/BeaverTails and generations
from a held-in harmful-response model, conditioned on the
identical state and filtered for intent match, recovery-branch safety, length
compatibility, and non-degeneracy. Mining uses training prompts only, and its
screening pairs are disjoint from the fresh pair later built for training, so
no development or test state is ever searched or trained on.

\subsection{Local Preference Objective}
\label{sec:local-objective}

Training uses the reference-adjusted branch score
\begin{equation}
  A_\theta(s,y) = \Ssum{\theta}(s,y)-\Ssum{\mathrm{ref}}(s,y),
  \label{eq:training-score}
\end{equation}
with \(\Ssum{\theta}(s,y)=\sum_{j}\log\pi_\theta(y_j\mid s,y_{<j})\) the
\emph{summed} branch log-likelihood---matching canonical DPO---and
\(\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}\) the frozen instruction checkpoint. We use canonical
DPO's summed continuation score rather than a length-normalized score; the
length-normalized average of Eq.~\eqref{eq:diagnostic-score} is used only for
the reference-free diagnostic margins. We set \(\beta=0.1\) for both the
diagnostic scale and the preference logit. The local loss is
\begin{equation}
  \begin{aligned}
    &\ell_{\mathrm{state}}(s_t,r_t,u_t)\\
    &\quad=
    -\log \sigma\!\left(\beta\left[A_\theta(s_t,r_t)-A_\theta(s_t,u_t)\right]\right).
  \end{aligned}
  \label{eq:state-loss}
\end{equation}

\paragraph{What is and is not new.}
Equation~\eqref{eq:state-loss} is canonical DPO on continuation pairs that share
their conditioning state. Scoring the shared prefix explicitly instead of
masking it leaves the preference difference and its gradient unchanged
(Appendix~\ref{app:algorithm}). The contribution is the pair
construction and state selection, which
Section~\ref{sec:results-factorial} tests.

\subsection{Lower-Tail State Aggregation}
\label{sec:tail-objective}

Let \(\mathcal{H}(x)\) contain the \(K=2\) states with the largest current
losses among a prompt's six states. The tail objective is
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{tail}}
    =
    \frac{1}{|\mathcal{H}(x)|}\sum_{s\in\mathcal{H}(x)}\ell_{\mathrm{state}}(s,r,u).
  \label{eq:tail-loss}
\end{equation}
All six losses are computed in one forward pass; the two indices are selected
from \emph{detached} values and gradients flow only through the selected
losses, so top-\(k\) selection is a non-differentiable masking operation.
Selection is recomputed whenever the prompt is sampled.
Section~\ref{sec:results-ablations} sweeps \(K\) and softmax-weighted
alternatives.

\subsection{Anchored Objective}
\label{sec:anchored-objective}

\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{L}
  =
  \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{tail}}
  +\lambda_{\mathrm{c}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{clean}}
  +\lambda_{\mathrm{b}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{benign}}.
  \label{eq:full-loss}
\end{equation}
Both auxiliary terms are Eq.~\eqref{eq:state-loss} applied to root-conditioned
pairs from the sets of Section~\ref{sec:models-data}, and neither is subject to
the top-\(k\) selection (Appendix~\ref{app:algorithm}). Batches are
stratified 60/20/20 over unsafe-state, clean-safety, and benign loss units, with
each component normalized within its stratum. One unsafe-state unit contains
the six pairs for one prompt, whereas each anchor unit contains one pair. We select
\((\lambda_{\mathrm{c}},\lambda_{\mathrm{b}})\) from
\(\{0,0.1,0.25,0.5,1.0\}^2\) on development by minimizing middle-insertion attack
success subject to three design constraints: clean attack success does
not exceed the base checkpoint, over-refusal rises by at most \(2\) points, and
IFEval falls by at most \(0.8\) points; ties favor the smaller
\(\lambda_{\mathrm{c}}+\lambda_{\mathrm{b}}\). This selects
\((\lambda_{\mathrm{c}},\lambda_{\mathrm{b}})=(0.25,0.5)\), frozen before any
test evaluation (Section~\ref{sec:results-ablations}).

\section{Experimental Design}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Models, Data, and Training}
\label{sec:models-data}

Our primary model is Qwen3.5-9B, evaluated with thinking disabled and text-only
inputs \citep{qwen2026qwen35}; we replicate on Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct
\citep{dubey2024llama3}. All trainable methods update the full model in BF16
under one recipe---effective batch \(96\), a fixed shared optimizer-step
schedule, a \(2048\)-token limit, a development-selected learning rate of
\(8\mathrm{e}{-7}\), and three seeds
(\(13,42,73\)) on \(4\times\)A100 80GB (Appendix~\ref{app:training}).

The corpus holds \(6{,}000\) training, \(750\) development, and \(1{,}000\) test
prompts, plus \(12{,}000\) clean-safety
and \(12{,}000\) benign-anchor pairs. Only training prompts carry same-state
triples---six each, \(36{,}000\) in all; development and test prompts are only
evaluated, never mined. Training prompts come from WildJailbreak
\citep{jiang2024wildteaming}, PKU-SafeRLHF/BeaverTails
\citep{dai2023saferlhf,ji2023beavertails}, and SORRY-Bench
\citep{xie2025sorrybench}; test prompts from HarmBench
\citep{mazeika2024harmbench}, StrongREJECT \citep{souly2024strongreject},
AdvBench \citep{zou2023gcg}, and JailbreakBench \citep{chao2024jailbreakbench};
benign anchors from UltraChat \citep{ding2023ultrachat}, Alpaca
\citep{taori2023alpaca}, and OR-Bench-80K \citep{cui2025orbench}. Counts,
licenses, deduplication, and split isolation are in Appendix~\ref{app:data}.

\subsection{Unsafe-State Suites}
\label{sec:state-suites}

A recurring weakness of mid-generation safety evaluation is that the unsafe
states are all inserted by the experimenter. We therefore evaluate on four
suites, only the first of which matches the training construction.

\textbf{(S1) Constructed insertions} place a harmful partial continuation at a
training-grid depth. \textbf{(S2) Unseen depths} use offsets disjoint from that
grid, extending to \(\{128,256,512\}\) tokens---far beyond the \(80\)-token
training cap---to test length extrapolation. \textbf{(S3) Naturally occurring
states} are not inserted at all: each evaluated checkpoint generates freely on
the same jailbreak prompts, and we truncate its generation at the first span an
independent evaluator flags as
unsafe (Llama Guard 3 8B, \citealp{dubey2024llama3}, none of the four judges of
Section~\ref{sec:statistics-human}). It also includes tool/RAG-contaminated
states and multi-turn drift over three to six turns
(Appendix~\ref{app:attacks}).
\textbf{(S4) Adaptive attack} optimizes against the defended checkpoint rather
than Base, combining RAP \citep{vega2025presto} with a prefill-position search
that maximizes the defended model's own harmful-branch preference, at a
\(256\)-query budget (Appendix~\ref{app:attacks} adds \(64\) and \(128\)).
Suites S2--S4 are never used for training or mining, and S3 is rebuilt per
checkpoint so it reflects that checkpoint's own failure modes.

\subsection{Baselines}
\label{sec:baselines}

\paragraph{Internal factorial.}
To attribute the gain we cross pair construction (same-state vs.\
root-conditioned) with objective (DPO, SimPO, recovery SFT), holding prompts,
branch texts, scored tokens, optimizer steps, model capacity, and seeds fixed.
The root-conditioned cells reuse the identical branch texts but remove the
reached state from the conditioning context, so the only difference is
\emph{what the two alternatives are conditioned on}. We also include
Worst-Insertion SFT and Clean DPO (root responses only) as endpoints, and a
prefix-masking control that scores the shared state explicitly, to confirm the
algebraic equivalence of Appendix~\ref{app:algorithm} empirically.

\paragraph{External defenses.}
We reproduce five published defenses, in six configurations, under the same
full-parameter recipe, prompt sources, attack protocols, and evaluators: deep
safety alignment \citep{qi2024safety}, reproduced for its data-augmentation
component only; backtracking \citep{zhang2024backtracking}; circuit
breakers \citep{zou2024circuit}; dual-objective refusal optimization in base and
weighted forms, DOOR and W-DOOR \citep{zhao2025door}; and PRESTO
\citep{vega2025presto}. Reproduction fidelity checks and hyperparameters are in
Appendix~\ref{app:baselines}.

\subsection{Metrics}
\label{sec:metrics}

\textbf{Clean \asr{}} is the fraction of root-prompt trials judged harmful
(\(n=1{,}000\) prompts). \textbf{Middle \asr{}} is the fraction of
mid-generation intervention trials that end in additional harmful content, over
\(n=4{,}940\) trials for S1 (valid states by depth:
\(1{,}000/998/994/985/963\)), \(2{,}081\) for S2
(three unseen depths, deeper states being rarer), \(870\)--\(930\) accepted
states per checkpoint for S3, and \(250\) for S4, one fixed stratified subsample reused
across methods and seeds (Appendix~\ref{app:attacks}). \textbf{Recovery rate} is
measured on its own suite of \(1{,}000\) reached states
(Appendix~\ref{app:attacks}), so it is not the complement of middle \asr{}. It is
the conjunction of three separately checkable conditions: the continuation adds
no harmful instruction (the \asr{} classifiers), begins recovery within \(64\)
generated tokens, a threshold fixed in advance, and issues a refusal, risk
explanation, or safe redirect (the WildGuard response-refusal field,
\citealp{han2024wildguard}). Beyond the binary rates we report additional
harmful tokens, \emph{time-to-recovery}, and a \(0\)--\(3\)
\emph{severity-weighted leakage} score (Appendix~\ref{app:recovery-quality}).
The S3 rate is conditional on a state being generated and accepted
(Appendix~\ref{app:attacks}).

\textbf{\orr{}} is the fraction of benign prompts incorrectly refused (XSTest
\citep{rottger2024xstest}, OR-Bench-Hard \citep{cui2025orbench}); utility uses
IFEval \citep{zhou2023ifeval} and MMLU-Pro \citep{wang2024mmlupro}. The primary
ASR columns use the HarmBench classifier \citep{mazeika2024harmbench};
StrongREJECT
\citep{souly2024strongreject}, WildGuard \citep{han2024wildguard}, and a
fourth LLM judge (\texttt{gpt-5.5-2026-04-23}) rescore the same outputs for
evaluator sensitivity. Full evaluator identities and decision rules are given
in Appendix~\ref{app:attacks} and are held fixed across methods.

\subsection{Statistics and Human Evaluation}
\label{sec:statistics-human}

We report two uncertainty estimates separately rather than pooling them: a seed
standard deviation over the three runs, and a prompt-clustered paired bootstrap
(\(10{,}000\) replicates, \(95\%\); Appendix~\ref{app:confidence-intervals}).
Confirmatory tests are
H1 (Section~\ref{sec:ltrm}) and \textbf{H2}: \emph{under matched supervision,
\method{} attains lower middle \asr{} than uniform same-state training.}

Because every safety number comes from a classifier, we rescore every condition
under all four judges of Section~\ref{sec:metrics}. The main
method ordering and stated contrasts are directionally consistent across
all four. We additionally hand-label \(150\)
outputs to anchor the judges against human labels
(Appendix~\ref{app:human-validation}).

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

\begin{figure*}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig3_diagnostics.pdf}
  \caption{(a) AUROC with \(95\%\) prompt-clustered intervals under four
  separation regimes; \ltrm{} leads every predictor in every regime. (b) Attack
  success by recovery-margin bin under the fully separated regime, with bin
  counts, intervals, and the fitted logistic curve.}
  \label{fig:diagnostics}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Root Safety Misses Recovery Vulnerability}
\label{sec:results-diagnostic}

Under the identical-state control, 47.9\% of examples with a positive root
safety margin have a negative same-state recovery margin at Base;
full-parameter clean-prompt DPO reduces this only to 41.3\% and recovery SFT to
32.4\%, whereas \method{}, which optimizes this quantity directly, reaches
16.1\% (Figure~\ref{fig:reversal}b). All four rates are read on the \(1{,}000\)
held-out test prompts over the frozen diagnostic pool of
Appendix~\ref{app:pairs}, never on a trained or mined state. The unit is a
\((\text{prompt},\text{state})\) pair, so this is the chance that a
\emph{particular} state inverts the root ordering, not that some state of a prompt
does, which would be higher. Consistent with H1, \ltrm{}\(_{20}\) measured on the
base checkpoint predicts held-out attack success better than root-level and
heuristic diagnostics (Figure~\ref{fig:diagnostics}a): under the fully separated
protocol its AUROC gain over the root margin is \(+0.131\) (\(0.779\) vs.\
\(0.648\); \(95\%\) CI \([0.109, 0.154]\)), and \(+0.163\) in-source. Attack
success is monotone in the margin (Figure~\ref{fig:diagnostics}b), and the
quintile gap persists after exact matching on depth bin and harmful-response
source, indicating that these two factors do not account for the gap.

A diagnostic computed from the same branch pool that constructs the attacks risks
predicting itself, so we validate \ltrm{} under four separations of increasing
strictness---in-source; cross-branch; cross-attack; and fully separated, with
disjoint branch source, attack family, and independent judge
(Appendix~\ref{app:diagnostics}). The fully separated regime is the primary
comparison. A control that sees the same states but aggregates
them uniformly reaches \(0.736\), so \(+0.088\) of the gain over the root margin
comes from conditioning on states at all and \(+0.043\) from their lower tail.

\subsection{Main Comparison}
\label{sec:results-main}

Table~\ref{tab:main-results} reports the primary model. Clean DPO lowers
clean-prompt attack success to 2.2\% yet leaves middle-insertion attack success at
52.4\%: a stronger root refusal does not solve recovery. Ranking the recovery
branch against its competing harmful future matters more than maximizing the
recovery target alone (Uniform Same-State vs.\ Recovery SFT, \(-11.3\)~pp), and
lower-tail aggregation adds more (H2: \method{} vs.\ Uniform, \(-5.9\)~pp,
\([-7.9, -3.9]\)).

DOOR and W-DOOR
\citep{zhao2025door} reinforce a safe continuation and unlearn harmful knowledge
but do not directly optimize a pairwise preference between continuations from
the identical state. W-DOOR is the strongest reproduced external comparator;
the internal factorial more directly isolates pairwise ranking. PRESTO
\citep{vega2025presto} trains against a rank-based rather than a
probability-based safety target and closes less of the gap.

