\subsection{What Causes the Gain}
\label{sec:results-factorial}

Table~\ref{tab:factorial} crosses pair construction with objective. Two effects
separate cleanly. Moving the conditioning context from the root prompt to the
reached state changes middle \asr{} by \(26.5\)~pp under DPO, \(22.5\) under
SimPO, and \(19.9\) under SFT: root-conditioned supervision, whatever objective
carries it, leaves most of the mid-generation failure untouched.

The second contrast concerns pairwise ranking at a fixed conditioning context.
Changing only the objective moves middle \asr{} by \(4.7\)~pp at the root and
\(11.3\)~pp at the reached state. Recovery SFT raises the likelihood of a
designated safe continuation without ranking it against the competing harmful
future and has \(11.3\)~pp higher \asr{} than same-state DPO. SimPO's per-branch
length normalization, which breaks the prefix cancellation
of Appendix~\ref{app:algorithm}, likewise costs \(1.5\)~pp at the root but
\(5.5\)~pp at a reached state. In this factorial, objective choice has a larger
effect under reached-state conditioning; lower-tail state selection adds
\(5.9\)~pp (Section~\ref{sec:results-main}). The prefix-unmasked control matches
its masked twin to within \(0.1\)~pp, consistent with masking being an
implementation choice rather than the source of the gain.


\subsection{Robustness}
\label{sec:results-robustness}

Depth extrapolation (S2) extends the maximum reached-state depth from \(80\) to
\(128\)--\(512\) tokens. \method{} achieves 26.0\% against Base's 63.1\%, and
38.7\% against 74.6\% at the
deepest \(512\)-token states (Appendix~\ref{app:depth} resolves both by depth).
Naturally occurring states (S3) remove the grid entirely and are regenerated per
checkpoint, so each defense is scored against its own failure modes: \method{}
reaches 19.6\% against Base's 49.3\%, and 24.6/14.8/19.2\% on the self-generated,
tool/RAG, and multi-turn families. The ordering is unchanged on the
accepted-state-weighted prompt rate (17.1\% against 45.8\%;
Appendix~\ref{app:attacks}). The adaptive attack (S4) probes whether the
observed robustness persists against checkpoint-specific optimization:
\method{} rises from 24.0\%
at \(64\) queries to 33.1\% at \(256\), against Base's 59.5\% and 69.0\%.
The ordering also transfers to Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct with nothing re-tuned:
24.7\% against Base's 64.6\% (Appendix~\ref{app:second-model}).

\subsection{Ablations and Over-Refusal}
\label{sec:results-ablations}

Appendix~\ref{app:ablations} sweeps the aggregation rule and the mining budget.
Concentrating on the tail beats averaging---top-2 reaches 20.2\% middle \asr{}
against 26.1\% for the mean of all six states---while performance is less
sensitive to the exact \(K\) and mining budget. Each anchor prevents one failure being traded for
another: on \emph{development}, where the weights were selected, dropping the
clean-safety anchor raises clean \asr{} from 4.4\% to 6.3\%, and dropping the
benign anchor pushes \orr{} from 7.8\% to 11.3\%.

Relative to Base, \method{} changes \orr{} from 6.5\% to 7.6\%, IFEval from
89.3 to 88.3, and MMLU-Pro from 77.6 to 77.0; its \orr{} remains below every
reproduced external defense (8.3--8.9\%). It has the lowest mean ASR under all
four judges (Appendix~\ref{app:human-evaluation}) and beats W-DOOR on every
unsafe-state suite, by \(2.9\)~pp on S1. On the recovery-quality suite,
\method{} scores \(0.55\) on the \(0\)--\(3\) leakage-severity scale against
W-DOOR's \(0.63\) and Base's \(1.38\), and has a mean seed-wise conditional
median time-to-recovery of \(10.3\) tokens against \(11.7\) and \(19.7\)
(Appendix~\ref{app:recovery-quality}); Table~\ref{tab:qualitative-full} gives
one recovery and one over-refusal transcript. The anchor constraints of
Section~\ref{sec:anchored-objective} bind only on development: clean \asr{} and
\orr{} also satisfy them on test, whereas the IFEval drop is \(0.7\) points on
development but \(1.0\) on test, \(0.2\) past the \(0.8\)-point band.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

Root-level refusal does not determine whether a model recovers from an unsafe
generation state. Holding that state fixed exposes a common preference
reversal that clean-prompt training only weakly addresses, and the lower-tail recovery
margin predicts held-out attack success far better than root-level diagnostics.
\method{} turns that diagnosis into a training signal by changing what the model
compares, not how it is scored.

\section*{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

Our threat model concerns recovery from unsafe generation states, not
prevention of every jailbreak. \ltrm{} and the recovery margins are predictive
statistics over sampled states, not certificates: they bound nothing over the
full reachable-state space, and an adversary can construct states outside our
sampling distribution. The adaptive attack of Section~\ref{sec:state-suites}
narrows this gap but does not close it: it adapts to the defended
\emph{checkpoint} rather than to the defense, so an attacker who used a recovery
margin to search directly for weakly recovered states is a stronger threat model
that we leave to future work.

The harmful and recovery branches are constructed alternatives. Although we
filter for intent match, audit a sample of pairs, and evaluate on an unseen
harmful-response source, the harmful source and the recovery policy jointly
shape what ``recovery'' means, and a different source could shift both the
mined hard states and the learned behavior.

Our scope is two English, text-only instruction models (Qwen3.5-9B and
Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct), one full-parameter recipe, and three seeds. Three
seeds support only weak seed-level inference, which is why we report seed and
prompt-level uncertainty separately instead of pooling them into one interval.
We do not evaluate other languages, modalities, larger models, or
reasoning-style generation.

Depth extrapolation is tested to \(512\) tokens; behavior at the multi-thousand
token states typical of long agentic traces is untested. The naturally
occurring suite depends on an evaluator to locate the first unsafe span, so
states whose harmfulness is encoded or metaphorical are under-represented in
it---the same blind spot that appears in our failure analysis.

Our over-refusal measurement covers behavior decided at or near the root of a
short response: \orr{} on XSTest and OR-Bench-Hard, plus IFEval and MMLU-Pro. One
failure mode specific to same-state training therefore falls outside it---a model
taught to abandon a trajectory it has begun could in principle abandon a
\emph{benign} long-form generation mid-way, where the response starts correctly
and truncation happens later. Measuring mid-generation behavior on benign
long-form prompts is left to future work.

In our experiments, stronger safety carries a measurable helpfulness cost.
\method{} increases
over-refusal on benign professional prompts containing high-risk terminology
(Table~\ref{tab:qualitative-full} shows one such case), and tightening safety
further would raise that cost. We report over-refusal
and utility next to every safety number so the trade-off stays explicit.

\section*{Ethical Considerations}
\label{sec:ethics}

This work uses harmful prompts and constructed harmful continuations solely to
study and improve recovery. We build on the public safety datasets named in
Appendix~\ref{app:data} and respect their licenses, including the non-commercial
research restriction on PKU-SafeRLHF, BeaverTails, and Alpaca. The method and its
diagnostics are dual-use, since a margin that guides safety training could in
principle be inverted to locate exploitable states; we therefore report aggregate
statistics rather than attack recipes and keep all per-state mining artifacts and
harmful branch pools internal. The two generations quoted in
Appendix~\ref{app:failures} are the only model outputs reproduced in the paper,
and their operational content is replaced by bracketed redactions. All labeling was carried out by the authors, so no
external annotators were exposed to harmful text, and no personal or user data
was collected.

% References must precede the appendices.
\bibliography{custom}

\appendix

\section{Algorithm, Masking, and the DPO Equivalence}
\label{app:algorithm}

\paragraph{One optimization step.}
Given a harmful prompt \(x\), its six same-state pairs
\(\{(s_k,r_k,u_k)\}_{k=1}^{6}\), and the clean and benign anchors: compute the
six state losses in a single forward pass; select the two largest
\emph{detached} losses; average their same-state preference losses; add the
clean-safety and benign-anchor terms; update the policy while keeping
\(\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}\) frozen. Top-\(k\) selection is a non-differentiable
masking operation, so gradients flow only through the two selected state losses
and the two anchor terms.

\paragraph{Sequence scoring.}
For a reached state \(s\) and branch \(y=(y_1,\ldots,y_{|y|})\) we form
\(s\oplus y\) and read per-token log-probabilities from one forward pass. Only
branch positions contribute: every prompt and state token carries the ignore
label \(-100\), and \(y\) is scored by \(\sum_j \log\pi_\theta(y_j\mid
s,y_{<j})\). The training score \(\Ssum{\theta}\) is this sum; the diagnostic
score \(\Savg{\theta}\) is the same quantity divided by \(|y|\).

\paragraph{Shared-prefix cancellation.}
The implemented objective masks \(s\) and directly computes the branch-only
score of Eq.~\eqref{eq:training-score}. In the unmasked control, the identical
tokenization of \(s\) contributes the same additive term to both summed sequence
scores and cancels from \(A_\theta(s,r)-A_\theta(s,u)\). This equivalence relies
on summed scores: per-sequence length normalization would divide the two
sequences by different lengths and prevent exact cancellation. The
behavioral difference between \method{} and a canonical response-level DPO comes
from (i) the pair construction that fixes \(s\) and varies only the future and
(ii) the aggregation over states. The prefix-unmasked row of
Table~\ref{tab:factorial} verifies this empirically, matching its masked twin to
within \(0.1\)~pp.

\paragraph{Anchor losses.}
Both auxiliary terms are reference-based preference losses rather than SFT, NLL,
or an explicit token-wise KL penalty. Each is Eq.~\eqref{eq:state-loss} with the
reached state replaced by the bare prompt \(x\):
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{clean}}
  =
  \mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{clean}}}
  \left[\ell_{\mathrm{state}}(x,y^{+},y^{-})\right],
  \label{eq:anchor-loss}
\end{equation}
with \(\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{benign}}\) defined identically on
\(\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{benign}}\). Expanding \(A_\theta\) gives the usual
\(-\log\sigma(\beta[\Delta_\theta-\Delta_{\mathrm{ref}}])\) form, where
\(\Delta\) is a difference of summed continuation log-probabilities. Both
anchors use the same frozen \(\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}\), the same prompt masking,
and the same \(\beta\) as the same-state loss. The reference stays at the
initial instruction checkpoint for the whole run: neither anchor uses a
separately updated reference, and neither receives the lower-tail top-\(k\)
selection applied to unsafe-state pairs.

\section{Same-State Pairs and Pair-Quality Audit}
\label{app:pairs}

\paragraph{A schematic pair.}
The template below is written for illustration rather than quoted from a run.
Shared state \(s_t\), already partly complying:
\texttt{User: [harmful request]} / \texttt{Asst: ``Sure---here is how.
First,''}. Recovery branch \(r_t\): \texttt{``---on second thought, I can't
help; here is a safe alternative\ldots''}. Harmful branch \(u_t\):
\texttt{``---you will need [redacted]\ldots''}. Both branches decode from the
byte-for-byte identical \(s_t\); \method{} ranks \(r_t\) above \(u_t\)
\emph{from this shared state}, whereas root-level training only refuses the
request up front.

\paragraph{Branch sampling.}
Recovery branches are sampled from the frozen instruction checkpoint at
temperature \(0.7\), top-\(p\) \(0.95\), top-\(k\) \(50\), and at most \(192\)
new tokens, stopping at the model EOS token. Harmful branches are taken first
from the public PKU-SafeRLHF/BeaverTails responses; intents for which no
length-compatible public branch exists are filled by an \(8\)B instruction
model initialized from Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct and given one epoch of
full-parameter SFT on the held-in harmful-response split, sampled at the same
settings except temperature \(0.8\). After a hard state is selected during
mining, we construct a fresh pair at that state that the search never used,
without reusing a screening pair for training.

\paragraph{Acceptance screen.}
Intent preservation is scored by the public NLI checkpoint
\code{MoritzLaurer/DeBERTa-v3-large-mnli-fever-anli-ling-wanli}: a harmful
branch is retained when it entails the original harmful intent with probability
at least \(0.80\) and contradiction probability at most \(0.10\). Two branches
are length-compatible when \(0.67\leq|r|/|u|\leq 1.50\) and
\(\bigl||r|-|u|\bigr|\leq 64\) tokenizer tokens. A recovery branch is retained
only when the HarmBench label is \texttt{No}, WildGuard's harmful-response
field is \texttt{no}, and its StrongREJECT score is below \(0.35\); a harmful
branch is retained when HarmBench returns \texttt{Yes} and either WildGuard's
harmful-response field is \texttt{yes} or its StrongREJECT score is at least
\(0.50\). This asymmetry keeps the safe-side screen conservative without
requiring unanimity on borderline harmful branches. Retained branches carry at
least \(16\) continuation tokens, with the floor waived only for a branch that
already completes a refusal and safe redirect, and must pass fixed degeneracy,
verbatim-copy, and malformed-output filters.

\paragraph{Fixed training and diagnostic pools.}
Training texts and recovery-margin diagnostic branches are generated once and
frozen across models. Thus Figure~\ref{fig:reversal}b changes only the scoring
model, not the ranked branches. The diagnostic pool is built on the \(1{,}000\)
test prompts under the acceptance screen above and never supplies a training
loss, so the reversal rates of Section~\ref{sec:results-diagnostic} and the
AUROC values of Appendix~\ref{app:diagnostics} are both measured out of sample.
Checkpoint-specific S3 states and adaptive S4
attacks are instead constructed under the common protocols of
Appendix~\ref{app:attacks}.

\paragraph{Yield and audit.}
Hard-state mining yields 396{,}000 valid paired evaluations from 526{,}931
attempted generations (75.2\%). Pair construction, which produces the 36{,}000
training triples, accepts 67.7\% of 53{,}184 attempted pairs. The four filters
run in the order intent mismatch, unsafe recovery branch, length
incompatibility, degeneracy, and the first failing condition supplies the
rejection category, so the four rates, each as a fraction of attempted pairs,
partition the rejected \(32.3\%\): 11.7\%, 9.0\%, 6.6\%, and 5.0\%
respectively. The screen is automatic on every pair and
is validated on a \(100\)-pair
sample independently audited by two authors. In that sample, 89 pairs satisfy
both human-audit criteria: the recovery branch is safe and helpful in 93 pairs,
and the harmful branch preserves the original harmful intent in 95, with
pair-level \(\kappa=0.79\).

\section{Data Sources, Licenses, and Split Audit}
\label{app:data}

\paragraph{Named sources.}
Table~\ref{tab:data-sources} lists every prompt source with its split
assignment and count. The design rule is that the training/development pool
and the test pool are drawn from disjoint families of resources: training uses
safety corpora excluded from evaluation, and testing uses the four
published harmful-behavior suites the safety literature scores on. No source
appears on both sides.

