  benign-anchor pairs. The \(12{,}000\) clean-safety pairs are two per
  training prompt and introduce no additional source.}
  \label{tab:data-sources}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Anchor pair construction.}
A clean-safety pair is rooted at an unmodified harmful prompt. Its chosen
response is a refusal, a concise risk explanation, or a safe redirect, and its
rejected response is a length-compatible harmful answer from the same fixed
harmful-response pool as the same-state branches (Appendix~\ref{app:pairs}).
Each training prompt contributes two such pairs, one with a concise refusal and
one with a refusal plus a safe redirect, giving \(12{,}000\) pairs. A benign
pair is rooted at an UltraChat, Alpaca, or non-Hard OR-Bench prompt. Its chosen
response is the source dataset's helpful response after quality filtering, and
its rejected response is a fluent but unnecessary refusal sampled from the
frozen base checkpoint under a safety-biased system prompt, retained only when
WildGuard labels it a refusal and the harmfulness classifiers of
Section~\ref{sec:metrics} label it non-harmful. The benign anchor therefore
ranks helpful compliance above excessive refusal without introducing harmful
text.

\paragraph{Licenses and permitted use.}
Each source is used under the license attached to its public release and only
for defensive safety training. PKU-SafeRLHF, BeaverTails, and Alpaca are
used subject to their non-commercial research restrictions; the evaluation
suites, WildJailbreak,
SORRY-Bench, UltraChat, and OR-Bench are released under permissive research
licenses. We do not redistribute raw harmful continuations.

\paragraph{Deduplication and split isolation.}
We deduplicate in four passes: exact normalized text; harmful-intent cluster;
embedding-neighbor cluster at cosine \(0.95\) on a sentence encoder; and
harmful-source/template identity. Deduplication runs \emph{across} sources before
splitting and clusters are assigned atomically to one split, so no near-duplicate
crosses a boundary; matched training-pool prompts are dropped rather than test
prompts, keeping the test suites complete. Over-refusal anchors are sampled from
OR-Bench-80K with the OR-Bench-Hard evaluation prompts and their near-duplicate
clusters removed first, and XSTest is never used for training. After all four
passes the source/template-identity join between the training, development, and
test pools is empty.

\section{Training Configurations and Compute}
\label{app:training}

All trainable methods update the full model in BF16 with FP32 optimizer states,
using AdamW (\(\beta_1=0.9,\beta_2=0.95\), weight decay \(0.1\)), DeepSpeed
ZeRO-3, activation checkpointing, and global-norm clipping at \(1.0\). The peak
learning rate is selected on development from
\(3\mathrm{e}{-7}\) to \(1\mathrm{e}{-6}\); the selected
\(8\mathrm{e}{-7}\) uses \(5\%\) linear warmup followed by cosine decay to
\(10\%\) of the peak. Each device processes one loss unit per micro-batch, and
\(24\) accumulation steps on \(4\) devices give an effective batch of \(96\)
loss units. An unsafe-state unit contains all six same-state pairs, whose six
losses are evaluated together before the detached top-\(2\) mask is applied;
an anchor unit contains one preference pair. Each effective batch realizes the
60/20/20 unsafe-state, clean-safety, and benign mixture of
Section~\ref{sec:anchored-objective} to the nearest integer, with losses
normalized within stratum before applying the anchor weights.

Every run uses seeds \(13,42,73\) on \(4\times\)A100 80GB and follows the same
fixed optimizer-step schedule. Development evaluation is performed at four
equally spaced points for diagnostics, but there is no metric-triggered early
stopping: the reported checkpoint is always the final scheduled checkpoint.
Development data select only the learning rate and anchor weights
(Appendix~\ref{app:ablations}); no test metric enters model or checkpoint
selection. We set \(\beta=0.1\) for both the diagnostic scale and the preference
logit.

\paragraph{Sequence truncation.}
Token limits are applied after rendering the checkpoint-specific chat
template; tokenizer, special tokens, and chat template come from the same
pinned revision as the corresponding weights. We remove the oldest complete
conversation turns, one turn pair at a time, while preserving the final user
request, the complete reached assistant state, and both scored branches, so
chosen and rejected branches always share an identical retained prefix and are
never truncated independently. A pair that still exceeds \(2048\) tokens is
discarded outright, so every retained example contains the full reached state
and full scored continuation used by the loss; padding tokens do not contribute
to the loss.

All trainable baselines---internal and reproduced external---share the prompt
split, branch pool, total scored-token and optimizer-step budgets, capacity,
effective batch, base checkpoint, seeds, and fixed final-checkpoint rule.
Same-state methods additionally share all six states. Method-specific
conditioning contexts, objectives, and anchors are stated explicitly rather
than assumed shared. \method{} requires a custom trainer because its detached
per-prompt top-\(k\) mask is not implemented by stock preference-optimization
trainers; the canonical DPO baselines are cross-checked numerically against a
standard implementation.

Table~\ref{tab:gpu-hours} gives per-run training GPU-hours. Per-run training cost
is comparable to the baselines: \(66.7\) GPU-hours against \(80.2\) for W-DOOR.
Hard-state mining adds a one-time \(119.4\) GPU-hours, paid once on the frozen
base model and amortized over every same-state configuration. The factorial of
Section~\ref{sec:results-factorial} holds prompts, branch texts, scored tokens,
and optimizer steps fixed across cells, so its contrasts are not attributable
to unequal training budgets. The
mining budget can be cut to \(B{=}20\), for \(104.1\) GPU-hours end to end,
while reaching \(21.8\)\% middle \asr{}
(Appendix~\ref{app:ablations}).

