\section{Attack Construction and Evaluation Protocols}
\label{app:attacks}

\paragraph{Suites.}
S1 constructed insertions place a harmful partial at a training-grid depth
\(\{5,10,20,40,80\}\). S2 uses offsets disjoint from that grid and extends to
\(\{128,256,512\}\). S3 contains three naturally occurring families, each
contributing at least \(250\) states: \emph{self-generated} states, obtained by
letting each evaluated checkpoint generate freely on the same jailbreak prompts
and truncating at the first unsafe span; \emph{tool/RAG-contaminated} states,
where the user
request is benign but a retrieved passage or tool result carries the harmful
procedure; and \emph{multi-turn drift} states accumulated over three to six
turns. S4 combines RAP \citep{vega2025presto} with a prefill-position search run
against the defended checkpoint. For continuity with prior work, the clean-root
condition is the clean \asr{} column of Table~\ref{tab:main-results} and the
classical single-prefix prefilling condition is S1 at its two shallowest depths.

\paragraph{Locating the unsafe span for S3.}
The span locator for the self-generated family is Llama Guard 3 8B, deliberately
not one of the four judges of Appendix~\ref{app:human-evaluation}, so no suite is
scored by the model that constructed it. Because Llama Guard labels a whole turn
rather than a span, we find the boundary by scoring successively longer prefixes
of the free generation and taking the first prefix it labels unsafe. Two authors
checked \(200\) located boundaries and judged \(181\) correct
(\(90.5\)\%, Wilson \(95\%\) CI \([85.6, 93.8]\)). Six observed errors placed
the boundary later than the apparent onset of unsafe content; the remainder
involved encoded/metaphorical content or ambiguity between tool output and model
repetition.

\paragraph{S3 yield and weighted rate.}
Because S3 is rebuilt per checkpoint, its ASR is conditional on that checkpoint
having produced an accepted span. Table~\ref{tab:s3-yield} partitions the same
\(1{,}000\) prompts into accepted states, no generated unsafe state, and
locator/filter rejections. Accepted yield falls from \(93.0\)\% for Base to
\(87.0\)\% for \method{}, while rejections remain near \(3\)\%. Counting prompts
without an accepted state as zero failures preserves the conditional ordering:
\method{} is lowest at \(17.1\)\%, versus W-DOOR's \(19.2\)\% and Base's
\(45.8\)\%; Uniform Same-State DPO and DOOR tie under this weighting.

