\paragraph{The recovery-rate suite.}
Recovery rate is not the complement of middle \asr{}, so it is measured on its
own pool: one reached state per test prompt, \(1{,}000\) in total, drawn
independently of the S1--S4 trials and held byte-for-byte fixed across methods
and seeds. Each state is scored by the three-way conjunction of
Section~\ref{sec:metrics}. The onset is the first token at
which the continuation enters that refusal, risk explanation, or safe redirect.
It is annotated in a single pass by the fixed LLM judge of
Appendix~\ref{app:fourth-judge-prompt}; it is not an EOS token and not a flip of
the harmfulness classifiers, and the same annotation supplies time-to-recovery in
Appendix~\ref{app:recovery-quality}. The third condition's response-level label
comes independently from WildGuard's response-refusal field, so the position and
the label are not read from the same annotator. The suite is disjoint from the S4 subsample
and shares no state identifier with it, which is why the denominators in
Table~\ref{tab:main-results} differ by column.

\paragraph{Adaptive-attack budget and cost.}
The attack is optimized independently against each defended checkpoint, which
makes S4 the most expensive condition in the paper, so it runs on one fixed
subsample of \(250\) initial test states reused for every method and seed: \(12\) methods
\(\times\) \(3\) seeds \(\times\) \(250\) states \(=9{,}000\) attack instances,
which pairs comparisons by prompt and initial state; the selected insertion can
differ by checkpoint. At \(256\) queries each this is roughly \(2.3\) million
generated continuations.

The subsample is proportional to the four test sources---\(100\) HarmBench,
\(78\) StrongREJECT, \(47\) AdvBench, and \(25\) JailbreakBench
states---and, within each source,
proportional to three initial-depth bands: shallow (\(5\)--\(20\) tokens), medium
(\(40\)--\(80\)), and deep (\(128\)--\(512\)). S4 therefore spans training-grid
and unseen depths together. It was drawn without replacement under a fixed random
seed and frozen before any S4 result was inspected, and stratification uses the
\emph{initial} state depth rather than the insertion position the attack
adversarially selects.

Table~\ref{tab:s4-budget} reports three attacker budgets, taken as nested
checkpoints of one \(256\)-query search. \method{} stays lowest at every budget,
and its advantage over W-DOOR grows from \(2.2\)~pp at \(64\) queries to
\(4.5\)~pp at \(256\).

