\paragraph{Evaluator identities and decision rules.}
We use the standard text-only HarmBench implementation from
\code{centerforaisafety/HarmBench} with checkpoint
\code{cais/HarmBench-Llama-2-13b-cls} \citep{mazeika2024harmbench}: the
standard-behavior classifier prompt, greedy decoding, and one generated class
token, with a normalized first label of \texttt{Yes} counted as harmful and
\texttt{No} counted as safe. Malformed outputs, fewer than \(0.02\%\) of
evaluations, are conservatively counted as harmful.

StrongREJECT \citep{souly2024strongreject} uses the released fine-tuned
evaluator \code{qylu4156/strongreject-15k-v1}, which scores a response in
\([0,1]\) as the expected value over its five ordinal score tokens. For binary
ASR we fix the threshold at \(s_{\mathrm{SR}}\geq 0.50\) before inspecting any
\method{} output.

WildGuard \citep{han2024wildguard} uses checkpoint \code{allenai/wildguard}
under greedy decoding. The ASR decision uses the generated
\emph{response harmfulness} field, and the recovery criterion uses the
generated \emph{response refusal} field; a response is harmful or refusing
when the corresponding parsed value is \texttt{yes}, equivalent to a
\(0.5\) decision threshold on the normalized first-token logits of the two
legal labels. Malformed WildGuard
outputs are treated conservatively as harmful and non-refusing.

The fourth evaluator is \texttt{gpt-5.5-2026-04-23}, called with the reasoning
effort set to none, a \(32\)-token output cap, and a prompt that requires JSON
output. Because this model family accepts only default decoding settings, the
judge is instead held fixed by pinning the snapshot, the prompt, and the
reasoning effort. Its complete prompt and rubric appear in
Appendix~\ref{app:fourth-judge-prompt}. It assigns a \(0\)--\(3\)
leakage-severity score and derives a binary ASR label by the fixed rule
\(\mathrm{ASR}=\mathds{1}[\text{severity}\geq 2]\). This same snapshot serves
as the independent judge in the fully separated diagnostic; it is not used to
generate branches, locate S3 boundaries, select hyperparameters, or construct
attacks. For root-level responses the reached-state field is empty and the
entire assistant response is judged as the candidate continuation.

\paragraph{Judges and decoding.}
Every safety condition draws from the same \(1{,}000\)-prompt held-out test
pool. S1--S2 use fixed reached states, S3 rebuilds checkpoint-specific states
under a common protocol, and S4 uses the fixed \(250\)-state initial subsample
described above. All four judges rescore every condition under the identities
and decision rules above. Over-refusal uses XSTest and OR-Bench-Hard; utility
follows the official IFEval and MMLU-Pro protocols in the non-thinking
configuration described next. Safety generations are sampled at temperature
\(0.7\), top-\(p\) \(0.95\), top-\(k\) \(50\), and at most \(256\) new tokens,
and each of the three runs draws a different
generation seed; this is the source of the non-zero seed SD on suites whose
inputs are held fixed, including the untrained Base row. Evaluator prompts, model revisions, and
decision thresholds are fixed before any \method{} result is inspected.

\paragraph{Utility evaluation and thinking mode.}
Qwen3.5-9B emits a reasoning block by default. Every condition in this paper
disables it (\code{enable_thinking=False}), because each safety suite seeds
the assistant turn with a controlled prefix and a reasoning block placed before
that prefix would change the reached state. Utility is therefore measured in the
same non-thinking configuration as safety rather than in the configuration the
model card reports, and Table~\ref{tab:thinking-gap} gives both so that the
difference is auditable: disabling reasoning costs \(2.2\) IFEval and \(4.9\)
MMLU-Pro points on the untrained checkpoint. Every utility comparison we make is
within the non-thinking column, so the reported degradations are not confounded
with this offset.

