\paragraph{Scope of each reproduction.}
For \citet{qi2024safety} we reproduce the data-augmentation component and not the
token-wise constrained objective, which targets fine-tuning attacks rather than
mid-generation recovery. In a paired experiment, adding that objective lowers
middle \asr{} by \(1.2\)~pp on S1 and \(0.1\)~pp on naturally occurring states,
for \(19\)\% more compute, without changing the method ordering. The other
documented adaptation is the layer placement for circuit breakers, flagged in
Table~\ref{tab:repro}.

DOOR combines robust refusal training---next-token supervision toward a safe
refusal on harmful-prefix-augmented inputs \(x\oplus h_{\leq k}\) with \(k\)
drawn over our fixed-depth grid---with NPO-based targeted unlearning of the
matched harmful branch and a benign retention term. W-DOOR adds token-level
weighting that upweights critical refusal tokens. Both reuse our prompts,
reached states, harmful-branch pool, and benign anchors, with loss-balancing
weights selected on development under the same constrained protocol as our
anchor weights. Learning rate, token and step budgets, capacity, seeds, and the
fixed final-checkpoint rule are shared by every trainable row; defenses without free
loss-balancing weights run at the weights their released implementations use.

\section{Diagnostic Separations, Regression, and Matching}
\label{app:diagnostics}

Table~\ref{tab:diagnostics} evaluates \ltrm{} and five alternatives under four
separations of increasing strictness. The unit of analysis is an attack
instance rather than a prompt: each of the \(1{,}000\) test prompts contributes
several instances, one per attack condition, which is why the class counts
exceed the prompt count. Because instances are nested within prompts, the
bootstrap resamples \emph{prompts} and carries all of a prompt's instances
together, matching the prompt-level clustering of the contrast intervals in
Appendix~\ref{app:confidence-intervals}; an instance-level bootstrap would
ignore that dependence and report narrower intervals than the design supports.

\ltrm{}\(_{20}\) leads on both AUROC and
PR-AUC in every regime. Absolute AUROC falls from \(0.861\) to \(0.779\) as the
separations tighten while the root-response margin falls from \(0.698\) to
\(0.648\); the fully separated block is the primary comparison.

\paragraph{What the diagnostic is for.}
\ltrm{} scores a prompt by comparing a recovery branch against a harmful branch
at each of its states, so computing it requires a constructed harmful
continuation. It is therefore an \emph{offline audit} statistic---run before
deployment, on a prompt set an auditor controls, to rank prompt families by how
weakly a checkpoint recovers---and not a runtime detector, which would have to
score a state without knowing the harmful alternative. The AUROC values above
predict attack success on the same checkpoint the margins were measured on;
whether margins measured on one checkpoint transfer to another is a separate
question we do not test.

