\paragraph{Regression and matching.}
Table~\ref{tab:logit} regresses attack success on the standardized recovery
margin with state depth, the root margin, and source indicators held fixed. The
recovery margin keeps an odds ratio of \(0.34\) per standard deviation, so its
contribution is not explained away by depth or by root-level safety; deeper
states are independently riskier (\(1.32\)) and a stronger root margin helps only
weakly (\(0.80\)). Splitting the fully separated states into recovery-margin
quintiles, attack success runs from \(75.4\)\% in the weakest quintile to
\(12.7\)\% in the strongest, a \(62.7\)~pp gap. Repeating that comparison after
exact matching on depth bin and harmful-response source leaves \(70.0\)\%
against \(14.6\)\% (\(n=611\) per group, \(55.4\)~pp), and the trend across bins
is monotone. These establish
predictive association over the evaluated states, not a causal or certified
robustness guarantee.

