\section{Aggregation, Mining, and Anchor Ablations}
\label{app:ablations}

\paragraph{Aggregation.}
Table~\ref{tab:aggregation} sweeps the state-aggregation rule over the mean of
all six states, dynamic top-1 through top-4, softmax weighting
\(w_i=\exp(\ell_i/\tau)/\sum_j \exp(\ell_j/\tau)\) with
\(\tau\in\{0.1,0.25,0.5,1.0\}\), and fixed base-margin weighting. Three
readings matter. Any form of concentration beats the uniform mean, by
\(3.7\)--\(5.9\)~pp on middle \asr{}, and the differences among the
concentrating rules are small: top-1, top-3, and both low-temperature softmax
variants all sit within \(0.5\)~pp of top-2, so the result is not specific to
the exact \(K=2\) choice. Top-2 achieves the lowest middle \asr{} while avoiding
the larger ORR and utility costs of the most concentrated alternatives. Within
the top-\(k\) and temperature-controlled softmax families, stronger
concentration generally corresponds to higher churn, the per-step fraction of
the selected state set that changes.

