\paragraph{Mining.}
Table~\ref{tab:mining} compares hard-state selection strategies at matched budget
and then sweeps the budget itself. Allocating the budget adaptively is what
matters: at the same \(66\) evaluations per prompt, Successive Halving with
confirmation pulls reaches \(20.2\)\% where a single random extra position reaches
\(24.7\)\% and a single-pair argmin over all \(11\) candidates \(23.5\)\%, and it
agrees with exhaustive search on \(0.78\) of prompts against their \(0.19\) and
\(0.31\). The budget itself is flexible: \(B{=}20\) already reaches \(21.8\)\% for
\(37.4\) mining GPU-hours and retains most of the observed improvement, while the full \(B{=}66\) buys both the final \(1.6\)~pp and markedly
more stable selection (reselection agreement \(0.59 \to 0.76\)).

